 "news": {
  "date": "2026-10-07",
  "asof": "2026-10-08 20:22 UTC",
  "headline": "The ten-year touched its highest since 2002 \u2014 and the reason was French, Japanese and American debt at once",
  "standfirst": "Tuesday\u2019s relief in the long end lasted exactly one session. The ten-year spiked to 5.35% in the morning, up nearly eight basis points and its highest since 2002, before closing at 5.29% \u2014 a fresh 52-week high \u2014 with the thirty-year at 5.671% and the two-year falling again to 4.777%. The fifth steepening in six sessions, and this time the framing was global: rising yields worldwide, record public debt, and France\u2019s index down 1.2% amid mass protests over a budget whose \u20ac74.5bn of interest payments exceed its education spending. The September Fed minutes then said the committee expects another hike before year-end. Equities gave back the record: S&P \u22120.22%, Russell \u22121.31%, two-thirds of issues lower. In the book GOOG crossed +1R at 347.37, a third consecutive closing high, and the stop question parked on 21 September is live again; AAPL rose 0.91% to its best close since the Ternus news and OKTA was the only holding to fall, at +1.75R. Heat 3.05R against 3.0R, unchanged for a twentieth session.",
  "themes": [
   {
    "kicker": "What's next",
    "right": "CPI on the 13th \u00b7 then 28 October",
    "title": "One print, and the Fed's own minutes have raised what rides on it",
    "items": [
     {
      "tone": "c-cb",
      "tag": "CPI",
      "html": "<b>September CPI</b> \u2014 the only first-tier print before the Fed, and now the referee on minutes that promise a hike",
      "when": "Tue \u00b7 2026-10-13",
      "kind": "calendar"
     },
     {
      "tone": "c-fomc",
      "tag": "FOMC",
      "html": "<b>Fed decision</b> \u2014 the minutes expect another increase this year; October odds about 20%",
      "when": "Wed \u00b7 2026-10-28",
      "kind": "calendar"
     },
     {
      "tone": "c-cb",
      "tag": "GOOG",
      "html": "<b>Alphabet Q3 earnings</b> \u2014 the first read on $195-205bn of capex, now with 3.6GW contracted against it",
      "when": "Wed \u00b7 2026-10-28",
      "kind": "calendar"
     },
     {
      "tone": "c-cb",
      "tag": "AAPL",
      "html": "<b>Apple Q4 earnings</b> \u2014 the first iPhone 18 quarter and the first read on Ternus",
      "when": "Thu \u00b7 2026-10-29",
      "kind": "calendar"
     }
    ],
    "note": "<b>CPI on the 13th now carries more than it did on Tuesday.</b> The Fed's minutes say another hike is coming; two ISM prices indices are accelerating; and core PCE missed to the downside eight days ago. A hot CPI validates the minutes and the long end together, and the thirty-year at <b>5.671%</b> stops being a term-premium puzzle and becomes an inflation trade. A soft one puts the Fed's stated intention against its own data and leaves the global debt story as the only explanation standing \u2014 which is the version with no release to trade against it."
   }
  ],
  "ondeck": "<b>CPI on the 13th now carries more than it did on Tuesday.</b> The Fed's minutes say another hike is coming; two ISM prices indices are accelerating; and core PCE missed to the downside eight days ago. A hot CPI validates the minutes and the long end together, and the thirty-year at <b>5.671%</b> stops being a term-premium puzzle and becomes an inflation trade. A soft one puts the Fed's stated intention against its own data and leaves the global debt story as the only explanation standing \u2014 which is the version with no release to trade against it.",
